 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to requirements for notice to local governments, community organizations and other interested parties of the potential for significant service reductions at certain state-operated hospitals.",
 "title": "Relates to notice of the potential for significant service reductions to persons with mental illness",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S2836",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S2836-2017",
     "printNo": "S2836",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 218\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 2836, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to notice to\n      local  governments  of  the  potential  for  significant   service\n      reductions at certain state-operated hospitals\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  legislation  would  amend the existing law to set a maximum time\nframe within which local governments,  employees,  and  advocacy  groups\nmust  be given notice prior to significant service reductions at certain\nstate-operated hospitals run by the Office of Mental Health (OMH).\n \n  This bill is identical to a bill I vetoed last year, and as  such,  it\nsuffers  from  the  same  flaws. (Veto No. 291 of 2016). First, it would\nmake it virtually impossible for OMH to  take  the  necessary  steps  to\nreduce  inpatient  capacity,  thereby  wasting  resources  that could be\ndirected to  other  facilities  with  greater  need.  OMH  is  currently\nrequired  to  provide  notification of a potential reduction at least 12\nmonths prior to reducing the number of beds at OMH  facilities,  but  is\nnot  required  to  commence the reductions immediately in order to allow\nsufficient time to transition services safely. Under this bill, however,\nOMH would only have one month  to  commence  the  reductions  after  the\nstatutorily required 12-month notification period expired. If the facil-\nity  did not reduce services within that period, it would be required to\nissue a new notice, and then wait another 12 months to take action.  Not\nonly would  this  require  the  unnecessary  expenditure  of  additional\nresources,  but  it  would  prohibit  an  orderly transition and lead to\nconfusion among patients and staff.\n \n  Second, it would renege on multi-year agreements with the  Legislature\non  reducing  inpatient  capacity at these facilities. For the past four\nyears, the Legislature has agreed that OMH  can  only  reduce  inpatient\ncapacity after a certain percentage of beds have gone unfilled for three\nmonths,  regardless  of  when  the  service  reduction  notification was\nissued. No rationale has been provided for rescinding these  agreements,\nespecially  when  they  provide  a necessary balance between maintaining\nbeds and managing  state  resources.  For  these  reasons,  I  am  again\nconstrained to veto the bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
